  ├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mclear()[39m       │ Removes it — this is logout            │
  ├───────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153monChange(fn)[39m  │ Fires on cross-tab [38;5;153mstorage[39m events      │
  └───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────┘

  The stored value is a versioned record under key [38;5;153mappSession.identity[39m, not a bare string:

  { [36m"v"[39m: [32m1[39m, [36m"userId"[39m: [31m"alice"[39m, [36m"loginAt"[39m: [31m"2026-09-30T14:02:11.000Z"[39m, [36m"source"[39m: [31m"client-asserted"[39m }

  Two fields there are deliberate. [38;5;153mv[39m exists because [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m outlives deploys — without it, the first schema change
  meets records written by the old code and has no way to tell them apart. [38;5;153msource: "client-asserted"[39m is the trust
  marker: it records [3min the data[0m that no server vouched for this, so when the real backend lands it writes [38;5;153m"server"[39m and
  any consumer that cares about trust can discriminate. That's the seam I promised for the backend swap.

  [1mOne refinement to your choice.[0m You picked "login establishes the identity" over a [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter — I'd keep that
  outcome but have the [3msubmit handler[0m call [38;5;153mAppSession.set(result.user)[39m after [38;5;153mlogin[39m returns success, rather than [38;5;153mlogin[39m
  writing to storage itself. Same signature ([38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m, unchanged), same behavior, but it keeps the
  network-call function from also owning persistence — so when [38;5;153mlogin[39m becomes async and returns a real server ID, only
  the handler changes. If you'd rather [38;5;153mlogin[39m write directly, say so and I'll flip it.

  [1mSection 2 — Data flow and lifecycle[0m

  1. Submit handler validates ([38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, unchanged).
  2. [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m runs — still the stub, still returns [38;5;153m{ success, user }[39m.
  3. On success the handler calls [38;5;153mAppSession.set(result.user)[39m.
  4. Any later form reads [38;5;153mAppSession.getUserId()[39m — no import, no load-order concern beyond the script tag.
  5. A logout control calls [38;5;153mAppSession.clear()[39m.

  On logout: "with an explicit logout" isn't real unless something can invoke it, so the design includes a minimal
  [38;5;153m<button id="logout">[39m in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m wired to [38;5;153mclear()[39m. Not a styled UI — just an actual way to exercise the path.

  Does this core look right before I go on to error handling and the test setup?

